  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "installation",
      "text": "In 1851 Léon Foucault demonstrated Earth’s rotation at the Paris Panthéon using a 28 kg brass and lead sphere on a 67 m steel wire.",
      "sources": [
        "pantheon",
        "academy"
      ],
      "risk": "ordinary"
    },
    {
      "id": "traces",
      "text": "A point mounted to the sphere marked successive passes in a layer of sand, making the change in swing direction relative to the floor visible.",
      "sources": [
        "pantheon",
        "academy"
      ],
      "risk": "ordinary"
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism",
      "text": "The oscillation plane shifts relative to the building because the Earth rotates beneath the pendulum.",
      "sources": [
        "memoir",
        "academy"
      ],
      "risk": "ordinary"
    },
    {
      "id": "latitude",
      "text": "The oscillation plane shifts through one full rotation relative to the ground in approximately one day at the North Pole, more slowly at Paris, and not at all at the equator.",
      "sources": [
        "memoir",
        "academy"
      ],
      "risk": "ordinary"
    },
    {
      "id": "invitation",
      "text": "Invitations for the 1851 demonstration invited people to see Earth turn.",
      "risk": "ordinary",
      "sources": [
        "pantheon"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "demonstration",
      "text": "The experiment made the rotation of Earth visible indoors and attracted a crowd of Parisians.",
      "risk": "ordinary",
      "sources": [
        "pantheon",
        "memoir"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "narration": "One swinging ball made an entire building look like it was turning. It's 1851 in Paris. Léon Foucault invites people to see Earth turn. Inside, the stone building feels perfectly still. From the dome of the Panthéon, he hangs a brass and lead ball on a steel wire. It's 67 metres long. The ball weighs 28 kilograms. A pointer under the ball cuts a line in sand. On the next pass, that line looks almost identical. That's a terrible show if you leave early. But give the sand time. Each pass adds another line, slightly tilted from the last, until the marks spread into a fan. Something is turning. So did the ball turn, or did the whole building turn beneath it? The floor is moving under everyone the whole time. You see, picture a swing over a spinning turntable. The seat keeps going past the same spot on the wall. But the marks underneath slide sideways. Stand on the turntable and it looks like the swing changes direction. At the North Pole, the floor makes one full turn in about a day. In Paris the shift is slower. At the equator, it disappears. People came to see Earth turn. The best part of the show was under their feet.",
